### Tested versions

- Reproducible in 4.5 (.NET), 4.5.1 (.NET)

### System information

Godot v4.5.1.stable.mono - Windows 11 (build 26100) - Multi-window, 2 monitors - Vulkan (Mobile) - dedicated NVIDIA GeForce RTX 3050 Ti Laptop GPU (NVIDIA; 32.0.15.5597) - AMD Ryzen 7 5800HS with Radeon Graphics (16 threads) - 15.40 GiB memory

### Issue description

In C#, `RandRange()` under `Godot.GD` (i.e., `@GlobalScope`) has the signature of `double`, whereas `RandfRange()` under `RandomNumberGenerator` has the signature of `float`.

This is not the case in GDScript, as the random methods in the global scope are consistent with the ones under `RandomNumberGenerator` class by having exactly the same function names and signatures. Considering in most (or to say, modern) cases, `float` in GDScript is `double` in C#, the signature in the global scope makes more sense.

It is likely due to the API changes from Godot 3 to 4, where GDScript splits `rand_range` into `randi_range` and `randf_range`. In C#, the split is (again) merged to `RandRange()` with `int` and `double` overloads in the global scope, but the methods under `RandomNumberGenerator` don't, and somehow still uses the "old" signatures of `float`.

Although in 99.99% of the time this difference in API is trivial, I still expect consistency here. A quick fix without having breaking change might be adding `RandRange` (with `int` and `double` overloads) to `RandomNumberGenerator`.

### Steps to reproduce

```cs
using Godot;
using static Godot.GD;

public class TestClass {
  public RandomNumberGenerator rng = new();

  public void TestMethod() {
    double r1 = RandRange(1.0, 2.0);       // This is double
    float r2 = rng.RandfRange(1.0f, 2.0f); // This is float
  }
}
```

